\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
The goal of this project is to study the use of a running locally large language model for automatic semantic annotation of a corpus. The system brings together lexical data from WordNet with how a local Ollama model thinks based on context. For each concept in the corpus, the model picks the most fitting WordNet sense, assigns special semantic labels when no matching synset is found and scores the sentiment of the identified concept. Also the system automatically manages multi-word expressions and keeps detailed statistics for later analysis.\\

This project evaluates how the amount of surrounding context affects disambiguation quality. To do this, annotations generated from several window setups are compared with human-annotated gold standard data. The alignment quality is measured using a varied evaluation results that includes accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score.\\

This topic has already been studied by many researchers. Traditional WSD systems rely on supervised machine learning or methods that use lexical resources such as WordNet. I wanted to explore how Large Language Models can be applied to a real Natural Language Processing task. WSD is a challenging problem since the same word can have different meanings depending on the context. I was interested in finding out whether a locally running LLM could correctly identify these meanings using WordNet as a lexical resource. Another reason for this project was to gain practical experience with prompt engineering. \\

\section{Method}
First, the program reads the input corpus and extracts the concepts that need to be annotated. For each concept it searches WordNet and extracts several possible meanings (synsets). If no suitable WordNet synonym exists, the system can select one of several predefined semantic labels. The program then creates a context for the target concept, including the current sentence and surrounding sentences (it could be from 0 to 3 sentences). The context, target word and possible meanings are passed to LLM through Ollama. The model selects the most appropriate meaning based on the context and returns the result in JSON format. \\

The MWE has two strateges of processing in this code. The main challenge here is that phrases like "no longer" or "find out" have a unique meaning only when the words are together. The script finds a phrase in a file, it splits the phrase and extracts each dictionary definition for individual words. The model is then given this huge list. LLM analyzes the context, understands that the words belong together as one unit and actively ignore all irrelevant meanings of separate words.\\

The second option for MWE processing: the script extracts the target multi-word expression from the input file. It requires WordNet only for the exact phrase ("find out"), without splitting it into individual tokens. The LLM receives a highly targeted list of just 1-3 valid definitions, not plenty of irrelevant ones and selects the most accurate shade of meaning based on the context.\\

After assigning semantic labels, the system evaluates the lexical sentiment of each concept on a scale from -100 to +100. Finally, all annotations are saved to a new JSON file. To evaluate the approach I run the annotation pipeline with different context sizes (0, 1, 2 and 3 sentences) and compare the results using accuracy, precision, recall and F1-score metrics.\\

